 "captions": {
  "script": "This volcano bleeds blue fire. Glitch Diary, entry two. Kawah Ijen, in East Java, Indonesia. Sulfuric gases burst from the rock at up to six hundred degrees Celsius. They hit the air... and ignite. Electric blue flames, up to five meters tall. But that's not the strange part. Some of the gas turns into liquid sulfur, and it keeps burning as it flows downhill. It looks like blue lava. It isn't. And there's something even stranger. Right beside the fire sits the largest acidic lake on Earth. NASA puts its pH below zero point three. Stronger than battery acid. And yet, some microbes seem to survive in it. Would you stand at the edge? Comment below. Follow for the next entry of my glitch diary. Because this volcano...",
